{
  "title": "Focal Fibrocartilaginous Dysplasia",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pathology/8079/focal-fibrocartilaginous-dysplasia",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 22 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Focal Fibrocartilaginous Dysplasia is a rare benign bone dysplasia that leads to unilateral varus of the tibia, most commonly seen in infants.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made with radiographs showing an abrupt varus deformity at the metaphyseal–diaphyseal junction of the tibia with cortical sclerosis at the medial cortex.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is usually observation as the majority of lesions spontaneously correct with normal proximal tibial physeal growth. Rarely, surgical deformity correction is indicated in the presence of deformity progression.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "usually seen in infant or toddlers",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "most common location is **tibia**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "may also occur in humerus, forearm, phalanx, and femur",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "etiology and the pathogenesis of the deformity are unknown",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**infantile tibia vara**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "important to recognize this variation of infantile tibia vara as it can resolve without surgery",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "unilateral tibia vara",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "knee hyperextension with lateral thrust can be seen",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "abrupt varus at the metaphyseal–diaphyseal junction of the tibia",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "cortical sclerosis at the medial cortex",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "radiolucency may appear just proximal to area of cortical sclerosis corresponding to the fibrocartilaginous tissue",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Studies",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Histology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8079/images/focal%20fibrocartilaginous%20dysplasia%20histology.jpg",
              "caption": "shows histology for fibroscartilaginous dysplasia"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "prominent layers of collagenic fibrous tissue with thick dense fibrocartilaginous tissue",
              "table": null,
